# tools/s/shape_member_pedestal_witness.rish -- the museum pedestals whose number is a count of
# members, and the declarations those members live in, held to one reading.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/s/shape_member_pedestal_witness.rish
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. `src/shape/` is a structure museum: each pedestal is a Glow desk whose placard
# names a shape and displays one `example` value. Seventeen stand under one leg in
# tools/gen/chapter/src_first_resident_witness.rish. Three read the engine there and assert
# agreement -- the repair REDS %358 bought. Five read a published constant of
# brushstroke/brush_parse.rye, held by tools/s/shape_constant_pedestal_witness.rish on the lap
# before this one. THIS RUNG TAKES THE SIX THAT REMAIN whose number is a FACT about an engine,
# and closes that class. Of the three left over, `tilak-root-count` already stands on its own
# source under tools/t/tilak_root_count_witness.rish; `shape-surface-count` and
# `shape-pool-agent-slot` display a SAMPLE -- an illustrative `21` for a one-field `@u32` shape,
# and the single slot that opens the Pool room -- so each keeps its placard and has no engine to
# be held against.
#
# A COUNT HAS NO PUBLISHED NAME, which is why these six could not join the constant rung. There is
# no `pub const manifest_field_count` to read: the three fields of `ManifestEntry` ARE the fact,
# and the desk's `3` is a reading of them. So the scan names the KIND of member per pedestal --
# struct fields, constructor parameters, sibling modules, refusal sites, fixture lines -- five
# kinds for six desks, each one a reading a visitor can rerun by hand at the declaration.
#
# WHAT THE LAP FOUND, and repaired. Four of the six desks named no source at all: the number stood
# on the placard and a reader had no declaration to reach. `shape-manifest-field-count` cited a
# brief, `shape-tube-manifest-field-count` and `shape-grant-family-count` cited docs/TUBE.md, and
# `shape-mand-ring-count` cited its own rings in prose -- honest provenance, and none of it a path
# to the engine. All four now carry a `Source:` line naming the module and the declaration, and the
# scan refuses if one leaves.
#
# WHAT THE CONTROL FOUND, and it rewrote the scan. The grant-family selector first ENUMERATED the
# three family modules by name, so a fourth mold in the room could never be counted -- a reading
# able to answer only its own length, green forever. Case 8 planted the fourth and walked free. The
# selector names the family's shape now, `glow_*_grant.rye` and `glow_*_scope.rye`, and a fourth
# family is seen the lap it lands.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN, on metal.
#   Each of the six placards reads in the order src/shape/PLACARD.md seats.
#   Each desk NAMES its source, so the pairing is checkable at the pedestal rather than private.
#   Each declaration resolves -- present, and in a form the reading understands.
#   Each desk's `example` equals the count of its members.
#   Every reading refuses from its own side: fifteen plantings in a throwaway pen, each caught by
#   the reading it was planted against, and four honest trees walking free -- among them a mold
#   grown in Rye and on its desk together, which walks free because this scan carries no number of
#   its own, and a grant WITNESS added beside its mold, which counts as no new family.
#
# WHAT IS NOT PROVEN. Whether these are the right counts, which is each module's own design
# question. Whether the pedestals lower and run, which
# tools/gen/chapter/src_first_resident_witness.rish already drives the Zig toolchain for; repeating
# it would buy one fact twice at the price of a toolchain launch, and the process bill is the
# roster's largest single cost.

say "shape-members: Language EN -- the museum displays counts, and this proves each one is the count it claims."
say "shape-members: Style Gauge -- every figure is read from its declaration rather than remembered."
say "shape-members: Lens TAME -- say why: five kinds of member for six desks, because six desks genuinely count five different things."

say "shape-members: the six pedestals against their declarations..."
let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/s/shape_member_pedestal_scan.sh"]
say scan.out
assert scan.ok else "shape-members: a pedestal and its declaration disagree -- read the verdict line"
assert scan.out contains "verdict=agree" else "shape-members: every desk must show the count its declaration resolves to"
assert scan.out contains "pedestals=6" else "shape-members: six pedestals are read; a seventh pair is welcome and moves this line with it"
say "field GREEN -- six desks stand on what their sources declare"

say "shape-members: the source citations, named..."
assert scan.out contains "manifest_fields_names=yes" else "shape-members: the manifest desk names amphora/manifest_entry.rye, so a reader reaches the declaration in one step"
assert scan.out contains "tube_fields_names=yes" else "shape-members: the TUBE desk names linengrow/tube_manifest.rye"
assert scan.out contains "grant_families_names=yes" else "shape-members: the grant desk names its mold modules"
assert scan.out contains "mand_rings_names=yes" else "shape-members: the Mand desk names its ring modules"
say "citations GREEN -- four desks that cited a brief now cite an engine"

say "shape-members: the control, both ways..."
let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/s/shape_member_pedestal_control.sh"]
say control.out
assert control.ok else "shape-members: the control must complete"
assert control.out contains "welcomes=4" else "shape-members: an honest tree, a mold grown in both rooms, a witness beside its mold, and a fixture grown with its desk must all walk free"
assert control.out contains "refusals=15" else "shape-members: every reading must refuse from its own side"
assert control.out contains "wrong=0" else "shape-members: no case may land on the side it was not planted for"
assert control.out contains "control_verdict=ok" else "shape-members: the control must agree with itself"
say "RED and GREEN paths proven -- fifteen plantings refused, four honest trees welcomed"

say "GREEN: shape-members -- every numbered pedestal in the museum now reads its own engine."
